---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="headshot slide-in-block">
    <Image
      src="/img/headshot.jpeg"
      alt="Tyler Crosse"
      width={300}
      height={300}
    />
  </div>

  <section class="intro slide-in-block">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tyler Crosse</p>
    <h1>Engineer & researcher building reliable, interpretable ML systems.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      I bridge distributed systems craftsmanship with mechanistic interpretability and
      empirical AI safety. Right now I’m pairing my MSCS work at Georgia Tech with
      my role as a research fellow at the Supervised Program for Alignment Research
      (SPAR), running hands-on experiments that make model evaluation reproducible at
      scale.
    </p>
    <div class="quick-links">
      <a class="pill" href="/research">Research →</a>
      <a class="pill" href="/projects">Projects →</a>
      <a class="pill" href="/ideas">Writing →</a>
      <a
        class="pill"
        href="https://github.com/tylercrosse"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >GitHub ↗</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="now slide-in-block">
    <h2>Currently</h2>
    <ul class="now-list">
      <li>
        Building a goal-drift evaluation harness for reasoning models as part of the
        SPAR fellowship, instrumenting activation drift alongside behavioral metrics.
      </li>
      <li>
        Writing interpretability experiment logs and BlueDot eval notes to turn paper
        insights into practical measurement checklists.
      </li>
      <li>
        Exploring 2026 roles that compound research engineering skills—ML platform,
        interpretability tooling, or evaluation teams in industry labs, alongside
        fellowship applications.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="trajectory slide-in-block">
    <h2>Where I'm Heading</h2>
    <p>
      My long-term aim is to contribute to mechanistic interpretability and empirical
      safety teams like Anthropic, GDM, or Redwood Research. The near-term plan is a
      deliberate “career-capital” loop: sharpen large-scale ML engineering inside a
      high-bar environment while continuing public research work that keeps me close to
      safety communities.
    </p>
    <ul class="trajectory-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Fellowships & labs.</strong> Applying to MATS, SPAR, Constellation, and
        similar programs to stay grounded in research questions that matter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Industry experience.</strong> Targeting ML/SWE roles focused on model
        reliability, eval infrastructure, or interpretability tooling to expand my
        production instincts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Communities.</strong> Active in BlueDot AI Safety Evals, ARENA-style
        study groups, and open-source interpretability tooling—working in public keeps
        feedback loops short.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="journey slide-in-block">
    <h2>My Journey</h2>
    <p>
      My path to computer science has been anything but conventional. I started
      with a degree in <strong>Biomedical Engineering</strong> (VCU), planning to
      build medical devices. I was quickly drawn to software—shipping production
      systems, leading engineering teams, and learning how to make complex systems
      robust and maintainable.
    </p>
    <p>
      After nearly a decade in industry, breakthroughs like
      <a
        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlphaFold"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">AlphaFold</a
      > and the rapid scaling of language models crystallized something for me: machine
      learning wasn't just another tool—it was going to revolutionize science in
      ways I hadn't imagined. That pushed me to deepen my foundations and focus on
      <strong>alignment</strong>: making powerful systems <em>understandable</em>,
      <em>steerable</em>, and <em>trustworthy</em>. Grad school became a laboratory for
      pairing systems rigor with interpretability research, and it set the stage for
      contributing to safety labs full time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="experience slide-in-block">
    <h2>Experience</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="timeline-item current">
        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <h3>Georgia Institute of Technology</h3>
          <p class="company">
            MS Computer Science <span class="period">| 2024 - Present</span>
          </p>

          <p class="description">
            Specializing in Machine Learning and High-Performance Computing.
            Coursework: Operating Systems, Machine Learning, High Dimensional
            Data Analysis, Deep Learning, Reinforcement Learning. Building
            evaluation tooling for goal drift, maintaining research logs, and
            collaborating with safety reading groups to translate theory into
            experiments.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline-item current">
        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <h3>Supervised Program for Alignment Research (SPAR)</h3>
          <p class="company">
            Research Fellow <span class="period">| 2025</span>
          </p>

          <p class="description">
            Working under mentor guidance on mechanistic interpretability
            experiments: building the goal drift evaluation harness, maintaining
            reproducible research logs, and translating BlueDot eval discussions
            into actionable measurement plans.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <h3>Knowable</h3>
          <p class="company">
            Lead Software Engineer <span class="period">| 5 years</span>
          </p>

          <p class="description">
            Led the core engineering team for a legal knowledge platform using
            search, LLMs, and structured data. Built event-driven microservices
            on AWS (Lambda/SQS/SNS), OpenSearch, and PostgreSQL; improved CI/CD
            to cut deployment time by 40%; mentored engineers and balanced
            architecture with delivery. The culture of instrumentation,
            measurement, and clean interfaces still shapes how I design research
            tooling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <h3>Rosetta Stone</h3>
          <p class="company">
            Senior Software Engineer <span class="period">| 2 years</span>
          </p>

          <p class="description">
            Contributed to the language learning platform that's helped millions
            of people learn new languages. Focused on backend systems and API
            development, working with international teams across multiple time
            zones.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <h3>Virginia Commonwealth University</h3>
          <p class="company">BS Biomedical Engineering</p>

          <p class="description">
            Where it all started. Studied the intersection of engineering and
            medicine, but discovered my true passion lay in the computational
            side of solving complex problems.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="interests slide-in-block">
    <h2>What I'm Into</h2>
    <div class="interests-grid">
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>🧩 Mechanistic Interpretability</h3>
        <p>
          Sparse autoencoders, circuits, and tools to open up black boxes so we
          can understand and steer models.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>🎯 AI Safety & Alignment</h3>
        <p>
          Building systems that are steerable, reliable, and beneficial by
          design.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>🛡️ Robustness & Evaluation</h3>
        <p>
          Designing measurement suites for honesty, goal drift, and reasoning
          faithfulness under distribution shift.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>🔧 Systems for ML</h3>
        <p>
          Operating systems, distributed systems, and tooling that make ML
          research reliable, scalable, and repeatable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>📊 Interpretability Interfaces</h3>
        <p>
          Building dashboards, visualization layers, and UX that make internal
          model structure legible to researchers and decision-makers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>⚡ High-Performance Computing</h3>
        <p>
          Squeezing performance from hardware: GPU kernels, memory hierarchies,
          and distributed training pipelines.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-card">
        <h3>📚 Learning in Public</h3>
        <p>
          Sharing experiments, mistakes, and processes so others can build on
          them—and so collaborators can spot new angles.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- <section class="focus slide-in-block">
    <h2>Focus: AI Safety & Interpretability</h2>
    <p>
      I'm interested in methods that make models more understandable and
